{"id":30150586312438,"type":"song","name":"Atomic","artist":"Blondie","year":1980,"weight":15,"info":{"cover":"https://cdn-images.dzcdn.net/images/cover/cdc9d8ba7d6f9d8c40c5389bb4577409/250x250-000000-80-0-0.jpg","album":"Atomic/Atomix","yt":"O_WLw_0DFQQ"},"artist_ref":{"id":122349618079736,"name":"Blondie","info":{"img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Blondie1977.jpg?width=500"}},"labels":[],"genres":[],"lists":[{"id":179984359651109,"name":"UK nº1 · 1980s","src":"singles","grp":"uk_number_ones","list_year":1980,"rank":452,"year":1980,"note":"2 semanas","total":191,"scaruffi":null}],"podcast":[{"kind":"podcast","title":null,"body":"Pulled as a single from their 1979 album, *Parallel Lines*, in early 1980, \"Atomic\" barely reached the Top 40 in America, peaking at number 39. However, it was a smash hit in the UK, spending a fortnight at number one. The song was crucial for Blondie's direction in the new wave era, incorporating elements of surf rock and a cowboy-like twang, while being danceable without being disco, marking a commercial yet credibly rock sound for the band. \"What was most important about Atomic was how it pointed away forward for Blondie in the new wave era.\"","year":1980,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Hit Parade Music History and Music Trivia","episode":"This Ain’t No Party! Edition Part 2","url":"https://shows.acast.com/hit-parade/episodes/695d4f6b39d31c85883dc9de"},{"source":"Hit Parade Music History and Music Trivia","episode":"This Ain’t No Party! Edition Part 2","url":null}]}],"book_tracks":[{"id":30150586312438,"artist":"Blondie","artist_id":122349618079736,"title":"Atomic","year":1979,"ess":0,"yt":"O_WLw_0DFQQ","kind":"s","book":{"id":8,"title":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","url":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/"},"scene":"Música de baile para punks","scene_id":68180978384461,"scene_link":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/#dance-punk","chapters":["Sección 5.6"],"pages":[[1,133